var e=[{kind:`lesson`,id:`R1`,track:`rfid`,title:`Wire & talk`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Wire the reader and talk to its chip`,goal:`Its own firmware (rfid-lab-r1; every RFID lab has its own) with our own driver in kit/lib/rfid-kit: no RFID library, so every byte is ours to see. Eight wires put the RC522 on the S3's second SPI bus, so it never shares with the screen. The first conversation is with the chip itself, not a card: its version register, the self-test from the datasheet, and 3,200 bytes through its FIFO and back.`,goalLine:`Prove that the ESP32 and the reader chip talk to each other reliably over SPI, before any card is involved. Keep the card and the tag away for now.`,goalCheck:[`The monitor says <code>VersionReg 0x92: MFRC522 v2.0</code> (or 0x91, or a clone's value), not 0x00 or 0xFF.`,`<code>selftest</code> ends with <code>self-test passed: 64/64 bytes match</code> on a genuine NXP chip (91h/92h), or <code>self-test consistent: 2 runs, same 64 bytes</code> on a clone such as the FM17522 (88h/B2h).`,`<code>loopback</code> says <code>0 of 3200 bytes wrong</code>.`,`On the <b>chip</b> view: the square glows teal and the bus counters keep climbing.`],learn:[`SPI address byte`,`registers`,`FIFO`,`datasheet self-test`,`two SPI buses`,`reading a bus fault`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`RC 3.3V`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`15`,role:`RC SDA`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`16`,role:`RC SCK`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`17`,role:`MOSI`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`18`,role:`MISO`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`14`,role:`RC RST`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`13`,role:`IRQ`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`RC GND`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r1 -e rfid-lab-r1 -t upload`,wiring:{title:`RC522 wiring (8 new wires, the screen stays as it is)`,head:[`RC522 pin`,`ESP32-S3`,`Role`],rows:[[`SDA`,`GPIO 15`,`chip select (NSS) in SPI mode, not I2C data`],[`SCK`,`GPIO 16`,`SPI clock, from the ESP32`],[`MOSI`,`GPIO 17`,`data ESP32 → chip`],[`MISO`,`GPIO 18`,`data chip → ESP32`],[`IRQ`,`GPIO 13`,`optional: the chip says “done”`],[`GND`,`GND`,`the right header's top pin`],[`RST`,`GPIO 14`,`reset; low = power-down`],[`3.3V`,`3V3`,`the second 3V3 pin. Never 5V`]]},commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r1 -e rfid-lab-r1 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`off`,does:`Builds lab R1's firmware and writes it to the board (no new downloads: it only uses the display libraries you already have).`,see:`<code>Hard resetting via RTS pin…</code>. The screen starts on the <b>card</b> view: rings, “Hold a card or tag”.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`uv run pio device monitor`,where:`mac`,card:`off`,does:`Opens the serial monitor. Opening it restarts the board, so you see the start-up lines.`,see:`<code>RC522 on SPI3: SS 15 SCK 16 MOSI 17 MISO 18 RST 14 IRQ 13 · 4 MHz</code> then <code>VersionReg 0x92: MFRC522 v2.0</code> and the command list.`,fix:`<code>0x00</code>: the chip isn't powered or MISO is stuck low: check 3.3V, GND, RST. <code>0xFF</code>: nobody drives MISO: check MISO (18) and SDA (15).`},{command:`chip`,card:`any`,does:`Reads VersionReg (37h) once more: the 2-byte transfer shown above, live.`,see:`The same line as at start-up (<code>0x92</code>, or e.g. <code>0xB2: FM17522 (clone)</code>). Run it a few times, and again after moving the card around: it must never change.`,fix:`It changes, or turns into 00h/FFh after you touched the reader = a loose wire. Press the RC522's header in or solder it, then <code>reset</code>.`},{command:`reset`,card:`any`,does:`Pulses the RST wire (a hard reset of the chip), sets it up again and reads VersionReg. The firmware also checks VersionReg by itself every 2 s and does this on its own when the chip goes quiet.`,see:`<code>hard reset: VersionReg 0x92</code> (or your clone's value). If the chip had gone quiet: <code>chip back after a hard reset</code> = it had hung; <code>still 0x00 after a reset</code> = a wire.`,fix:`Still 00h/FFh: power off, press the header pins in (or solder them), check 3.3V, GND, MISO (18), SDA (15).`},{command:`selftest`,card:`any`,does:`The datasheet's digital self-test (section 16.1.1): resets the chip and has it compute a fixed 64-byte sequence. A genuine NXP chip (91h/92h) is compared with the datasheet's table. A clone has no table, so the test runs twice and the two runs are compared. Polling pauses while it runs, so the card can stay where it is.`,see:`4 rows of 16 bytes with no <code>!</code> marks, then <code>self-test passed: 64/64 bytes match</code> (NXP) or <code>self-test consistent: 2 runs, same 64 bytes</code> (clone). The chip view says <b>passed</b> or <b>consistent</b>.`,fix:`<code>self-test skipped: chip silent (0x00)</code> = the chip wasn't answering before the test even started: a wire, see <code>reset</code>. <code>!</code> marks = bytes that differ: re-seat SCK/MOSI/MISO and run again. <code>doesn't run the self-test</code> = some clones simply don't have it; loopback is the test that counts then.`},{command:`loopback`,card:`any`,does:`Writes 64-byte patterns into the chip's FIFO and reads them back, 50 times: 3,200 bytes each way.`,see:`<code>FIFO loopback: 0 of 3200 bytes wrong at 4 MHz</code> in teal at the bottom right of the screen.`,fix:`Any errors at 4 MHz = a bad contact on SCK, MOSI or MISO, or wires much longer than 20 cm.`},{command:`regs`,card:`any`,does:`Prints 15 registers with their bits and what they mean.`,see:`<code>VersionReg 92h</code>, <code>TxControlReg 83h</code> (antenna on), <code>RFCfgReg 40h</code> (33 dB gain), <code>TPrescalerReg A9h</code> + <code>TReloadReg 03 E8</code> (a 25 ms timer: 1,000 ticks of 25 µs).`,fix:`TxControlReg 80h = antenna off: the chip was reset without our set-up. Unplug and replug the board.`},{command:`spi 20, then loopback, then spi 4`,card:`any`,does:`Optional peek at the edge lab: runs the bus at 20 MHz, twice the chip's rating, then back to the default.`,see:`Maybe a few wrong bytes at 20 MHz, maybe none: it depends on your wires. The chip view's SPI clock turns amber above 10.`,fix:`Always finish with <code>spi 4</code>. The R6 edge lab measures this properly.`}],panels:[{title:`One register read, bit by bit`,widget:`rfidSpi`,intro:`Every command in this lesson is built from transfers like this one. It is what a logic analyser would show on the four SPI wires while the ESP32 reads one register: 16 clock ticks, two bytes. The first byte goes out on MOSI and says <b>which</b> register and <b>read or write</b>; during the second byte the chip answers on MISO. <code>chip</code> does exactly this with VersionReg. Use ◀ ▶ to step bit by bit, pick another register, or choose a fault to see what a loose wire turns the answer into: that is how you'll read 00h or FFh in your own monitor.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`rfidScreens`,view:0,intro:`The board's screen has six views. A click on the push button (or BOOT) goes to the next one, a double-click runs the action written at the bottom left, a long press pauses card polling. Pick a view to see what each part means; the numbers match the list below it.`}],steps:[`Unplug USB. Keep the screen wired as in lesson 03. Add the RC522 with 8 female-to-male jumpers as in the table: SDA, SCK, MOSI and MISO go to 15, 16, 17, 18, side by side in the same order as on the module.`,`Check twice: the module's 3.3V goes to <b>3V3</b>, never 5V. The MFRC522 runs on 2.5–3.6 V and its pins are not 5 V tolerant.`,`Flash, then open the monitor (the first two commands above). <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> VersionReg 0x92 (or 0x91, or a clone value).`,`Type <code>selftest</code>. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> “passed” (NXP chip) or “consistent” (a clone).`,`Type <code>loopback</code>, then <code>regs</code>. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> 0 bytes wrong, and the register values from the table.`,`Click to the <b>chip</b> view and compare it with “What the screen shows”: teal square, passed, 4 MHz, bus counters climbing. That's the goal. Lay the card on the reader for a second as a preview of R2: the on-board LED flashes bright green and stays green while it lies there.`],note:`The on-board RGB LED tells you what the reader sees: a bright green flash each time a card is read, steady green while one lies on the reader, red if the reader chip stops answering (a wire), off otherwise. Many RC522 boards come with the header loose: push-in pins lose contact, so solder it or press it in firmly. IRQ is optional: without it the firmware polls, and the chip view says “polling” (R6 compares both). The card's position doesn't change any of R1's results. But handling it near the reader is the moment loose header pins lose contact: if VersionReg turns 00h/FFh afterwards, the firmware notices within 2 s, tries a hard reset and tells you whether it was a hang or a wire.`,preview:{widget:`rfid`,view:0}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`R2`,track:`rfid`,title:`Wake a card`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`The field, and the first hello`,goal:`The reader's antenna makes a 13.56 MHz magnetic field. The card has no battery: its coil harvests that field, its chip wakes up, and it answers by loading the field in a rhythm the reader can hear. First in 3D, then frame by frame, then for real in the sniffer.`,goalLine:`See a card wake up and answer, read its identity, and find your reader's range.`,goalCheck:[`The card view shows your card's UID, “MIFARE Classic 1K”, ATQA 00 04 and SAK 08.`,`The trace view lists the wake-up frames: WUPA, ATQA, ANTICOLL, UID + BCC, SELECT, SAK, HLTA.`,`You know roughly at what height the read rate starts dropping (your range).`],learn:[`13.56 MHz near field`,`inductive coupling`,`100 % ASK pauses`,`load modulation`,`REQA · WUPA · ATQA`,`a polling task`,`sniffing frames`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`RC 3.3V`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`15`,role:`RC SDA`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`16`,role:`RC SCK`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`17`,role:`MOSI`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`18`,role:`MISO`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`14`,role:`RC RST`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`13`,role:`IRQ`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`RC GND`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r2 -e rfid-lab-r2 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r2 -e rfid-lab-r2 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`any`,does:`Flashes lab R2's own firmware: everything from R1, plus the trace view and the poll and trace commands. <code>help</code> lists exactly this lab's commands.`,see:`<code>rfid-lab-r2: reader task started</code>, then this lab's command list and views.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`(click to the card view)`,where:`button`,card:`off`,does:`One click on the push button or BOOT goes to the next view; the dots at the top show which one.`,see:`Rings around the antenna and “Hold a card or tag flat on the reader”, plus how many taps so far.`,fix:``},{command:`lay the card flat on the reader`,where:`hands`,card:`on`,does:`The reader task polls ~12 times a second; the first poll that finds the card wakes it, reads its UID, then halts it.`,see:`The on-board LED flashes bright green, then stays green while the card lies there. The card view shows the UID, type, ATQA/SAK and a read rate that fills to 100 %. The monitor prints <code>card 9C:4F:… · MIFARE Classic 1K · ATQA 0004 SAK 08</code> and the frames.`,fix:`Nothing? Run R1's <code>chip</code> again; then try the card flat in the middle of the antenna.`},{command:`lift the card slowly`,where:`hands`,card:`move`,does:`The field gets weaker with height; polls start to miss.`,see:`The read rate turns amber, then coral. After 3 misses in a row (~0.3 s) the monitor says <code>card removed</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`(click to the trace view)`,where:`button`,card:`any`,does:`The sniffer: the frames of the last wake-up, with times in ms.`,see:`<code>&gt; 52 WUPA</code>, <code>&lt; 04 00 ATQA</code>, <code>&gt; 93 20 ANTICOLL CL1</code>, <code>&lt; UID + BCC</code>, <code>&gt; SELECT CL1</code>, <code>&lt; SAK</code>, <code>&gt; HLTA</code>.`,fix:`“Sniffer off”: type <code>trace on</code>.`},{command:`trace off / trace on`,card:`any`,does:`The sniffer is on by default and prints each new card's frames in the monitor. Off silences the monitor and the trace view.`,see:`<code>sniffer off</code> / <code>sniffer on</code> at the bottom right.`,fix:``},{command:`gain 0, gain 7, then gain 4`,card:`move`,does:`Receiver gain 18 dB (0), 48 dB (7), default 33 dB (4). Hold the card at the edge of your range for each.`,see:`The read rate at the same height changes with gain, but only up to a point: far away the card has no power, whatever the gain.`,fix:`Finish with <code>gain 4</code>.`},{command:`poll off / poll on (or a long press)`,card:`any`,does:`Pauses and resumes card polling.`,see:`The card view says “polling paused”; new cards are ignored until <code>poll on</code>.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`The field, in 3D`,widget:`rfidField3d`,intro:`A model of your reader and card: drag to turn it, move the card up and down, change the gain, and press Send WUPA to watch one wake-up. The chart under it shows why the range ends where it does.`},{title:`Follow one wake-up, step by step`,widget:`rfidSeq`,flows:[`wake`,`select`],intro:`What happens in the first millisecond after a card arrives, from the ESP32's SPI writes to the card's answer. The same frames appear in your sniffer.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`rfidScreens`,view:1,intro:`The board's screen has six views. A click on the push button (or BOOT) goes to the next one, a double-click runs the action written at the bottom left, a long press pauses card polling. Pick a view to see what each part means; the numbers match the list below it.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as R1. Flash <code>rfid-lab-r2</code> (the command above), then click to the <b>card</b> view.`,`Lay the white card flat on the reader. <b>Checkpoint:</b> the LED flashes bright green and stays green; UID, MIFARE Classic 1K, read rate 100 %.`,`Click to the <b>trace</b> view and find the 7 frames; compare with the step-by-step diagram above.`,`Lift the card slowly and note the height where the read rate drops: your range.`,`Repeat at the edge of range with <code>gain 0</code> and <code>gain 7</code>, then <code>gain 4</code>. Compare with the 3D model's prediction.`,`Try the key tag: its own UID, same type.`],note:`The reader polls about 12 times a second in its own task on core 0, so the screen never waits. A card counts as removed only after 3 missed polls in a row (~0.3 s), so one weak poll at the edge of range doesn't make it flicker. The 3D model is simplified on purpose: measure your real range in R6.`,preview:{widget:`rfid`,view:1}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`R3`,track:`rfid`,title:`Who's there`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Two cards at once: anticollision`,goal:`Lay the card and the tag on the reader together. Both answer at the same instant, bit by bit, and where their UIDs differ the reader hears a collision. Anticollision walks down the bits like a binary tree: pick a branch, ask again with that prefix, select the card that's left, halt it, repeat until nobody answers.`,goalLine:`Find both cards at once, and point to the exact bit where their UIDs collided.`,goalCheck:[`<code>inventory</code> lists 2 cards with both UIDs.`,`It names the collision bit, and the walk-through above lands on the same bit with your two UIDs.`],learn:[`bit collisions`,`binary tree walk`,`NVB`,`BCC`,`cascade levels`,`SAK`,`HALT · WUPA`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`RC 3.3V`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`15`,role:`RC SDA`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`16`,role:`RC SCK`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`17`,role:`MOSI`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`18`,role:`MISO`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`14`,role:`RC RST`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`13`,role:`IRQ`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`RC GND`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r3 -e rfid-lab-r3 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r3 -e rfid-lab-r3 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`any`,does:`Flashes lab R3's own firmware: everything from R2, plus inventory (and a double-click on the card view runs it). <code>help</code> lists exactly this lab's commands.`,see:`<code>rfid-lab-r3: reader task started</code>, then this lab's command list and views.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`lay the card and the tag together`,where:`hands`,card:`both`,does:`Both cards wake on the same WUPA. The poll resolves the collision and selects one of them.`,see:`The card view shows one UID (always the same one) and an amber line: <code>collision at bit 23</code> (your bit will differ).`,fix:`Only one card found? Overlap them more: both must sit inside the antenna's loop.`},{command:`inventory (or double-click on the card view)`,card:`both`,does:`Switches the field off for 10 ms so every card restarts, then REQA → select → halt, again and again until nobody answers.`,see:`<code>#1 9C:4F:6A:0D … collision at level 1 bit 23 -&gt; chose 1</code>, <code>#2 9C:4F:2A:1B …</code>, then <code>inventory: 2 cards in the field</code>.`,fix:`<code>select failed (collision)</code>: move the cards a little and run it again.`},{command:`inventory with one card, then none`,card:`move`,does:`Same walk, nothing to resolve.`,see:`<code>1 card</code> without a collision line, then <code>0 cards</code>.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`Anticollision: a walk down the bits`,widget:`rfidAnticoll`,intro:`The two rows are the UID bits of two cards, in the order they travel (least significant bit of byte 0 first). Step through: where the bits differ the reader hears a collision, picks 1, and only the matching card keeps answering. Type your own UIDs from the monitor.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`rfidScreens`,view:1,intro:`The board's screen has six views. A click on the push button (or BOOT) goes to the next one, a double-click runs the action written at the bottom left, a long press pauses card polling. Pick a view to see what each part means; the numbers match the list below it.`}],steps:[`Flash <code>rfid-lab-r3</code> with the command above (same wiring as R1). <code>help</code> shows this lab's commands.`,`Lay the card and the tag on the reader together, overlapping.`,`Watch the card view: one UID, and the collision bit in amber.`,`Type <code>inventory</code> (or double-click on the card view). <b>Checkpoint:</b> 2 cards, and the collision bit.`,`Type both UIDs into the walk-through above and step to step 2: the collision lands on the same bit.`,`Leave both on the reader: the card view always shows the same card. The walk always picks 1 at a collision, so the same card wins every time.`],note:`Bits travel least-significant first, so “bit 23” is bit 6 of the third UID byte. Our driver is tested for two cards at once: with three or more, the chip's collision position after the first branch is harder to trust. 7-byte UIDs take two cascade levels; the first part starts with 88h, the cascade tag.`,preview:{widget:`rfid`,view:1}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`R4`,track:`rfid`,title:`Inside the card`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Open the card's memory`,goal:`A MIFARE Classic 1K holds 1,024 bytes: 16 sectors × 4 blocks × 16 bytes. Every sector has its own lock, the trailer: two keys plus access bits that say who may read or write each block. Open each sector of your own card with the factory key and dump it.`,goalLine:`Dump your own card's whole memory and explain block 0 and one sector trailer byte by byte.`,goalCheck:[`<code>dump</code> ends with <code>dump: 16 sectors read</code>, and the memory view is all coloured (no grey “closed” sectors).`,`In the dump you can point to the UID in block 0 and to <code>FF 07 80 69</code> in a trailer, and the decoder above explains what FF 07 80 allows.`],learn:[`sectors & blocks`,`sector trailers`,`access bits (stored twice)`,`3-pass authentication`,`encrypted frames`,`manufacturer block`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`RC 3.3V`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`15`,role:`RC SDA`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`16`,role:`RC SCK`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`17`,role:`MOSI`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`18`,role:`MISO`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`14`,role:`RC RST`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`13`,role:`IRQ`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`RC GND`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r4 -e rfid-lab-r4 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r4 -e rfid-lab-r4 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`any`,does:`Flashes lab R4's own firmware: everything from R3, plus the memory view, dump and read. <code>help</code> lists exactly this lab's commands.`,see:`<code>rfid-lab-r4: reader task started</code>, then this lab's command list and views.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`dump (or double-click on the memory view)`,card:`still`,does:`For each of the 16 sectors: authenticate with the factory key A, read its 4 blocks, decode the trailer. About 1.5 s; keep the card still.`,see:`64 lines (block, 16 bytes, ASCII, meaning), then <code>dump: 16 sectors read</code>. The memory view: block 0 amber, empty blocks dark, trailers coral.`,fix:`Grey “closed” sectors: their key isn't the factory key (a card someone set up): the lab leaves those alone. “no card”: it moved; run it again.`},{command:`read 0`,card:`on`,does:`Authenticates sector 0 and reads block 0 only.`,see:`UID (4 bytes), BCC, then 08 04 00 (SAK, ATQA), then the maker's bytes.`,fix:``},{command:`read 1 (with trace on)`,card:`on`,does:`The same for block 1, with the sniffer showing the frames.`,see:`<code>AUTH key A</code> marked as done inside the chip, then <code>READ</code> and 16 bytes, both “[encrypted on air]”.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`The card's memory, in 3D`,widget:`rfidMemory3d`,intro:`Each cabinet is a sector, each slab a block, the top slab the trailer with its lock. Click a sector, then Authenticate: only then can its blocks be read.`},{title:`Access bits decoder`,widget:`rfidAccess`,intro:`Bytes 6–8 of every trailer decide who may do what in that sector. Tick the bits or type the three bytes from your dump.`},{title:`Authenticate and read, step by step`,widget:`rfidSeq`,flows:[`read`],intro:`What one <code>read 4</code> does on the bus and in the air.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`rfidScreens`,view:3,intro:`The board's screen has six views. A click on the push button (or BOOT) goes to the next one, a double-click runs the action written at the bottom left, a long press pauses card polling. Pick a view to see what each part means; the numbers match the list below it.`}],steps:[`Flash <code>rfid-lab-r4</code> with the command above (same wiring as R1). <code>help</code> shows this lab's commands.`,`Hold the white card on the reader and keep it still. Type <code>dump</code> (or double-click on the memory view). <b>Checkpoint:</b> 16 sectors read.`,`In the dump, read block 0: UID, BCC, SAK, ATQA, maker's bytes.`,`Find a trailer (blocks 3, 7, 11…): key A reads as 00 (the card never reveals it), then <code>FF 07 80 69</code>, then key B.`,`Type <code>FF 07 80</code> into the access-bits decoder above: data blocks 000, trailer 001.`,`<code>read 1</code> with the trace view open: the AUTH and the encrypted READ.`],note:`Authentication runs inside the MFRC522 (its MFAuthent command): the ESP32 hands over the key and never sees the handshake, and the key never goes into the trace. The firmware only uses the factory key, on cards you own, and never writes block 0 or a trailer: a trailer with broken access bits locks its sector for good.`,preview:{widget:`rfid`,view:3}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`R5`,track:`rfid`,title:`Write & count`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Write a label, keep a count`,goal:`Write your own 16 bytes into a data block and read them back. Then use MIFARE's built-in arithmetic: a value block stores a number three times (once inverted) and the card itself adds or subtracts, then commits the result in one step. A backup block keeps the value from before.`,goalLine:`Write text to your card and keep a credit counter on it that the card itself updates.`,goalCheck:[`<code>write 4 hello bench</code> says “written and read back”.`,`After <code>credits init 50</code> and <code>credits use 3</code>, the credits view shows 47 with backup 50.`,`<code>dump</code> shows block 4 as data and blocks 8 and 9 as value blocks.`],learn:[`block writes · ACK / NAK`,`read-back checks`,`value block format`,`INCREMENT · DECREMENT · TRANSFER`,`backup blocks`,`guard rails`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`RC 3.3V`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`15`,role:`RC SDA`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`16`,role:`RC SCK`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`17`,role:`MOSI`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`18`,role:`MISO`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`14`,role:`RC RST`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`13`,role:`IRQ`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`RC GND`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r5 -e rfid-lab-r5 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r5 -e rfid-lab-r5 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`any`,does:`Flashes lab R5's own firmware: everything from R4, plus the credits view, write and credits. <code>help</code> lists exactly this lab's commands.`,see:`<code>rfid-lab-r5: reader task started</code>, then this lab's command list and views.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`write 4 hello bench`,card:`on`,does:`Opens sector 1, checks its access bits allow key A writes, writes 16 bytes (text padded with zeros), reads them back.`,see:`Block 4's bytes, <code>|hello bench.....|</code> and <code>written and read back</code>.`,fix:`<code>READ BACK DIFFERS</code>: the card moved mid-write; run it again.`},{command:`write 3 test`,card:`any`,does:`Tries to write a sector trailer.`,see:`Refused before anything is sent: <code>not writable here (block 0 and trailers are off limits)</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`credits init 50`,card:`on`,does:`Writes 50 as a value block into block 8 and the same into the backup, block 9.`,see:`The credits view: 50, backup 50, a coin stack.`,fix:``},{command:`credits use 3 (then credits add 10)`,card:`on`,does:`Copies the value into the backup, then the card subtracts (or adds) and commits with TRANSFER.`,see:`47, backup 50; then 57, backup 47.`,fix:`<code>no credits on this card yet</code>: run credits init first.`},{command:`credits use 999`,card:`on`,does:`The firmware checks the balance first.`,see:`<code>only 57 credits, can't use 999</code>: nothing is sent to the card.`,fix:``},{command:`credits`,card:`on`,does:`Reads blocks 8 and 9 and decodes them.`,see:`The current value and the backup.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`A value block, byte by byte`,widget:`rfidValue`,intro:`How the card stores a number so that it can check it itself. Change the value and watch which bytes move.`},{title:`credits use 3, step by step`,widget:`rfidSeq`,flows:[`credits`],intro:`The four card commands behind one <code>credits use 3</code>.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`rfidScreens`,view:4,intro:`The board's screen has six views. A click on the push button (or BOOT) goes to the next one, a double-click runs the action written at the bottom left, a long press pauses card polling. Pick a view to see what each part means; the numbers match the list below it.`}],steps:[`Flash <code>rfid-lab-r5</code> with the command above (same wiring as R1). <code>help</code> shows this lab's commands.`,`Card on the reader. <code>write 4 hello bench</code>. <b>Checkpoint:</b> written and read back.`,`<code>write 3 test</code>: refused, a trailer. So is block 0.`,`<code>credits init 50</code>, then <code>credits use 3</code>. <b>Checkpoint:</b> 47, backup 50.`,`<code>credits add 10</code>: 57, backup 47. Step through the diagram above to see the four card commands.`,`<code>dump</code> again: block 4 is data, blocks 8 and 9 are value blocks.`],note:`Credits on a MIFARE Classic are a learning toy, not money: anyone with the key can rewrite the block. Payment and transit systems use cards with modern cryptography (such as MIFARE DESFire) and check every transaction on a server.`,preview:{widget:`rfid`,view:4}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`R6`,track:`rfid`,title:`Edge lab`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Find the limits on your own bench`,goal:`Seven experiments that each push one setting until something breaks: the SPI clock, receiver gain against distance, how fast the card answers, how long each step takes, polling against the IRQ wire, the chip's CRC unit against the ESP32, and hundreds of reads in a row. Each one draws a chart on the screen and prints a table you can paste below.`,goalLine:`Measure where your own bench breaks, and compare it with the datasheet.`,goalCheck:[`You know the highest SPI clock with 0 errors, your card's reply time, and the read success rate held still.`,`Your tables are pasted into the Experiments panel above, next to the example.`],learn:[`measured vs datasheet`,`SPI signal integrity`,`timeouts & reply time`,`IRQ vs polling`,`offloading`,`reliability statistics`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`RC 3.3V`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`15`,role:`RC SDA`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`16`,role:`RC SCK`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`17`,role:`MOSI`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`18`,role:`MISO`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`14`,role:`RC RST`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`13`,role:`IRQ`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`RC GND`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r6 -e rfid-lab-r6 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r6 -e rfid-lab-r6 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`any`,does:`Flashes lab R6's own firmware: everything from R5, plus the edge view and the seven edge experiments. <code>help</code> lists exactly this lab's commands.`,see:`<code>rfid-lab-r6: reader task started</code>, then this lab's command list and views.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`edge spi`,card:`any`,does:`FIFO loopback at 1 to 40 MHz, then a chip reset. About 1 s.`,see:`A table of % wrong per MHz and a bar chart; the 10 MHz bar is outlined (the datasheet limit).`,fix:``},{command:`edge crc`,card:`any`,does:`300 checksums by the chip and by the ESP32.`,see:`0 mismatches; the chip much slower (SPI round trips).`,fix:``},{command:`edge irq`,card:`any`,does:`30 WUPAs polling, 30 sleeping on the IRQ wire. Run it with the card on, then off.`,see:`SPI accesses per command for each; the gap grows a lot with no card.`,fix:`“IRQ never fired”: the IRQ wire (13) is loose.`},{command:`edge timeout`,card:`still`,does:`Shrinks the chip's wait timer from 25 ms to 40 µs. About 5 s.`,see:`100 % down to ~100 µs, then 0 %: the card's reply time.`,fix:`All 0 %: the card wasn't on the reader.`},{command:`edge timing`,card:`still`,does:`40 full cycles, each step timed.`,see:`min / avg / max µs for WUPA, SELECT, AUTH, READ.`,fix:`“no complete cycle”: hold the card still.`},{command:`edge gain`,card:`still`,does:`30 tries at each gain step, at one height. Run it close, then at ~3 cm.`,see:`% found per gain: all 100 close up; low gains fail first further away.`,fix:``},{command:`edge stress 500`,card:`move`,does:`500 full read cycles (~10 s). Lift or tilt the card slowly while it runs.`,see:`Counts per outcome: ok, absent, CRC, protocol… and reads per second.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`Experiments`,widget:`rfidEdge`,intro:`One tab per experiment: what it pushes, what to look for, the example result, and a box for your own table.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`rfidScreens`,view:5,intro:`The board's screen has six views. A click on the push button (or BOOT) goes to the next one, a double-click runs the action written at the bottom left, a long press pauses card polling. Pick a view to see what each part means; the numbers match the list below it.`}],steps:[`Flash <code>rfid-lab-r6</code> with the command above (same wiring as R1). <code>help</code> shows this lab's commands.`,`Card on the reader and kept still (a book on top helps). Click to the <b>edge</b> view: double-click there runs the next experiment.`,`<code>edge spi</code>, <code>edge crc</code>, <code>edge irq</code> (card on, then off).`,`<code>edge timeout</code> and <code>edge timing</code> with the card still. <b>Checkpoint:</b> your card's reply time.`,`<code>edge gain</code> close, then at about 3 cm.`,`<code>edge stress 500</code> while you slowly lift the card.`,`Paste each table into the matching tab above.`],note:`Double-click on the edge view runs the next experiment. edge spi resets the chip afterwards, in case a fast, garbled write changed a setting. Pull the IRQ wire and run edge irq: the firmware notices and falls back to polling.`,preview:{widget:`rfid`,view:5}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`R7`,track:`rfid`,title:`Card manager`,status:`on board, works`,heading:`Manage your cards from a React app`,goal:`Its own firmware (rfid-lab-r7) turns the bench into a door you manage from a small React app (esp-labs/apps/door-app) over your Wi-Fi. The board keeps the card list in its flash and decides every tap itself; the card only brings its UID. From the app you add cards, name them, give them a role, block them, limit them to hours, an end date or a number of uses, copy one card's rules to another, replace a lost card, write a name label onto them, wipe the data the labs wrote, save a card's data and write it back or copy it onto another card, open the door remotely, switch on lockdown, and watch every tap live. Two simulated locks (the screen and the LED): the front door, for every role, and the admin room, for admin cards only; the one reader is mounted at one of them at a time.`,goalLine:`Run your two tags as a small access system: one resident, one guest, and see each rule decide a tap.`,goalCheck:[`The app shows <b>live</b> and both tags are in the list: the white card as a resident, the key tag as a guest.`,`Tapping the white card shows OPEN on the screen and in the app; after <b>Block</b> the same tap shows DENIED · blocked.`,`The guest tag's uses go down with each tap; at 0 it is refused with “no uses left”.`,`Every tap and every change appears in the access log, on the board's log view and in the app.`,`The white card's data is saved, copied onto the key tag and compared: same data, and the key tag still opens as <b>Key tag</b>, because a copy never moves the UID.`],learn:[`REST API + WebSocket`,`server-side decisions`,`rule order`,`fail closed`,`NVS storage`,`admin token via a dev proxy`,`React + zod`,`backup, restore, verify`,`a copy is not a clone`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`RC 3.3V`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`15`,role:`RC SDA`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`16`,role:`RC SCK`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`17`,role:`MOSI`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`18`,role:`MISO`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`14`,role:`RC RST`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`13`,role:`IRQ`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`RC GND`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r7 -e rfid-lab-r7 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r7 -e rfid-lab-r7 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`any`,does:`Flashes the door firmware. The first build copies the web libraries W2 already uses (ESPAsyncWebServer, AsyncTCP, ArduinoJson) into esp-labs/.pio.`,see:`The screen shows <b>DOOR door</b>, LOCKED, “0 cards in the list” and <code>http://bench.local</code> at the bottom once Wi-Fi joins.`,fix:`“no Wi-Fi saved: run W1 once”: R7 uses the network W1/W2 saved. Flash W1, <code>join</code>, then flash R7 again.`},{command:`token`,card:`any`,does:`Prints the admin token (shared with W2). Every change in the app needs it.`,see:`<code>Admin token: 3f…</code> and where to put it.`,fix:`Keep it out of chats and git. <code>token new</code> replaces it.`},{command:`cd esp-labs/apps/door-app && cp .env.example .env`,where:`mac`,card:`any`,does:`Creates the app's settings file: <code>BENCH_URL=http://bench.local</code> (or the IP from <code>status</code>) and <code>BENCH_TOKEN=</code> the token.`,see:`A .env file next to package.json. It is git-ignored.`,fix:``},{command:`pnpm install, then pnpm dev`,where:`mac`,card:`any`,does:`Installs the app (React 19, Vite 8, zod: the same versions as the dashboard) and starts it. Vite forwards /api and /ws to the board and adds the token on the way, so the browser never holds it.`,see:`Open <code>http://localhost:5174</code>: the door panel says <b>live</b>, the chip says B2h · FM17522 (your clone), the clock shows the time.`,fix:`“connecting…” forever: is the board on Wi-Fi (screen footer)? Does <code>http://bench.local/api</code> open in the browser? FRITZ!Box: allow Wi-Fi devices to talk to each other (W2).`},{command:`Add card: “White card”, resident`,where:`app`,card:`on`,does:`The board waits 20 s for a card it hasn't seen; the screen shows ADD A CARD and the LED blinks blue. Then lay the white card on the reader.`,see:`A toast “White card added”, the card in the list with <b>opens now</b>, an “enrolled” line in the log.`,fix:`“already in the list”: that card is enrolled; tap another or delete it first.`},{command:`Add card: “Key tag”, guest`,where:`app`,card:`on`,does:`The same for the key tag. Guests start with 5 uses.`,see:`Key tag · guest · <b>5 uses left</b>.`,fix:``},{command:`tap each card`,where:`hands`,card:`move`,does:`The board decides from its list: OPEN 5 s (green LED, padlock opens) or DENIED 2 s (red LED, with the reason).`,see:`The app's door panel and log update within a second; the guest's uses go 5 → 4 → 3.`,fix:`Nothing happens, or “card can't be read”: look at the app's <b>Reader chip</b> cell and the banner under it. “settings restored” means the RC522 reset itself when its 3.3 V dipped (often while Wi-Fi transmits): short wires to the 3.3V pin, header pins pressed in or soldered. “IRQ wire stays quiet” is harmless: the board polls instead. Serial: <code>status</code> prints the same.`},{command:`Block, then Unblock`,where:`app`,card:`any`,does:`The quickest rule: a blocked card is refused before anything else is checked.`,see:`<b>blocked</b> on the card; its next tap shows DENIED · blocked.`,fix:``},{command:`Edit → presets`,where:`app`,card:`any`,does:`Office hours (08-18), Night shift (22-06, across midnight), Weekend guest (10 uses until Sunday 20:00), One visit (1 use, 24 h), Lost card (blocked). Save sends only what changed.`,see:`The card's rule line and its “opens now” chip change at once.`,fix:`“no clock yet”: dated or timed cards are refused until the board has internet time (fail closed).`},{command:`Add card: “Admin fob”, admin`,where:`app`,card:`on`,does:`A fourth role: admin cards open the front door like a resident, and they are the only cards the admin room opens for. They also open during lockdown.`,see:`The card's row shows <b>front door ✓</b> and <b>admin room ✓</b>; a resident's shows <b>admin room · admins only</b>.`,fix:``},{command:`Locks → Admin room → Move reader here`,where:`app`,card:`any`,does:`The bench has one reader, so it is mounted at one lock at a time (saved on the board). Serial: <code>at admin</code>, <code>at front</code>.`,see:`The admin room tile gets the teal border and “reader here”; the screen says ADMIN ROOM above the padlock.`,fix:``},{command:`tap the admin card, then a resident card`,where:`hands`,card:`move`,does:`Same board, same list, different lock: the role check comes right after “blocked”, before any hours or dates.`,see:`Admin card: the admin room OPEN 5 s. Resident card: DENIED · <b>admins only</b>, logged with “admin room”. Move the reader back: the resident opens the front door again.`,fix:`Still OPEN for a resident? Check which tile says “reader here”.`},{command:`open admin  (or Open 5 s on a tile)`,where:`serial`,card:`any`,does:`Opens one lock from the app or serial, whatever card is near; <code>open</code> alone is the front door.`,see:`That tile goes green for 5 s; the log says “opened from the app” with the lock.`,fix:``},{command:`Lockdown on`,where:`app`,card:`any`,does:`Only maintenance and admin cards open. The LED glows amber while idle.`,see:`LOCKDOWN on the screen, every resident and guest tap DENIED · lockdown.`,fix:``},{command:`Edit → Write name label`,where:`app`,card:`on`,does:`Writes the name into block 4 and the role into block 5 of that card (factory key, sector 1 only), then reads them back.`,see:`“label written”. Read the card on the reader: block 4 shows its name.`,fix:`The door still ignores the label: it decides from its own list. Write any name there and nothing changes.`},{command:`Edit → Wipe card data`,where:`app`,card:`on`,does:`Second click to confirm. Blocks 4-6 and 8-10 back to zeros where the access bits allow (the label, R5's credits). Keys, trailers and block 0 are never touched.`,see:`“6 of 6 data blocks cleared”.`,fix:``},{command:`Edit → Remove from the list`,where:`app`,card:`any`,does:`Second click to confirm. The board forgets the card; the card itself is not touched.`,see:`Its next tap: DENIED · unknown card.`,fix:``},{command:`Edit → Copy rules from…`,where:`app`,card:`any`,does:`Fills the form with another card's role, hours, end date and uses (not its name, not its block). Save sends what changed.`,see:`Both cards now have the same rule line.`,fix:``},{command:`Card data → Save the card on the reader`,where:`app`,card:`on`,does:`Reads blocks 4-6 and 8-10 with the factory key and keeps them on the board as a named snapshot (up to 8, in NVS). Serial: <code>save White</code>, <code>saved</code>.`,see:`“6 of 6 blocks saved as …” and a snapshot with its six cells: text, values (R5's credits), zeros.`,fix:`“no lab block opens”: that card's sectors 1-2 use another key: nothing to save.`},{command:`Card data → Download, Import a saved file`,where:`app`,card:`any`,does:`Downloads the snapshot as a JSON file on your Mac; Import puts such a file back on the board (lab blocks only, checked twice: by the app and by the board).`,see:`A <code>card-data-….json</code> file; after import, a snapshot “from a file”.`,fix:``},{command:`Card data → Copy this card onto another`,where:`app`,card:`move`,does:`Reads the card on the reader, then the screen shows COPY: 2ND CARD and the LED blinks violet. Lift it and lay the other card within 20 s: each block is written and read back. Serial: <code>copy</code>, <code>cancel</code>.`,see:`“6 of 6 blocks copied from White card onto 9C4F6A0D”, a “data copied here” line in the log.`,fix:`Laying the same card again does nothing (it waits for a different one). Cancel writes nothing.`},{command:`Compare with the reader`,where:`app`,card:`on`,does:`Reads the card on the reader and lines it up against a snapshot, block by block.`,see:`<b>same</b> for each copied block. Now tap the key tag: it still opens as <b>Key tag</b>.`,fix:``},{command:`Write onto the card on the reader`,where:`app`,card:`on`,does:`Second click to confirm. A snapshot back onto the card it came from (restore), e.g. after R5 spent its credits, or onto another card. Serial: <code>restore 1</code>.`,see:`“… written to 9C4F2A1B (the card it came from)”.`,fix:``},{command:`Edit → Replace with a new card`,where:`app`,card:`move`,does:`For a lost or broken card: the next new card you tap takes over its name, role and rules; the old one stays in the list, blocked, renamed “… old”.`,see:`Two entries: the new card opens, the old one shows DENIED · blocked if it ever turns up.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`Which rule decides?`,widget:`doorRules`,intro:`The board checks the rules in this order, and the first one that says no wins. Set up a card, the clock and lockdown, and see which rule decides. It is the same order as kit/lib/door-rules/src/access_rules.h (tested on the Mac).`},{title:`Follow one tap, one block, one new card, one copy`,widget:`doorSeq`,intro:`What crosses the wires and the Wi-Fi: the app never talks to the reader directly, and the card never decides anything.`},{title:`What a copy moves`,widget:`doorCopy`,intro:`Save the white card's data, copy it onto the key tag, and watch which blocks move. Block 0 (the UID) and the sector trailers (keys, access bits) never do, and a sector that doesn't open with the factory key is skipped, not forced.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as R1. Flash <code>rfid-lab-r7</code>. The board needs the Wi-Fi network W1/W2 saved.`,`In the monitor: <code>token</code>. Copy it into <code>esp-labs/apps/door-app/.env</code> as <code>BENCH_TOKEN=</code>.`,`<code>pnpm install</code> and <code>pnpm dev</code> in esp-labs/apps/door-app, open <code>http://localhost:5174</code>. <b>Checkpoint:</b> live, chip B2h, a clock.`,`Add the white card as a resident and the key tag as a guest. <b>Checkpoint:</b> both in the list, “opens now”.`,`Tap both a few times: OPEN, the guest's uses going down, every tap in the log.`,`Block the white card and tap it (DENIED · blocked), unblock it. Try the presets and lockdown, and watch the “Which rule decides?” panel agree.`,`Write a label onto a card, read it back, wipe it. Then remove the key tag from the list and tap it: unknown card.`,`Save the white card's data, copy it onto the key tag, compare. <b>Checkpoint:</b> same data on both, yet each card still opens as itself.`,`Download the snapshot, wipe the white card, write the snapshot back, compare: restored.`],note:`Why the board decides and not the card: a card's UID and the data on a MIFARE Classic can be read by anyone with a reader, so they are fine as a name tag, not as proof. Real access systems keep the decision on a server and use cards with modern cryptography (e.g. MIFARE DESFire); this lab teaches the server side of that. Reading the API is open on your Wi-Fi; every change needs the token. The card list lives in the board's NVS and survives re-flashing; it is separate from the R1-R6 labs. Copying moves data only: block 0 is written once at the factory, so the copy keeps its own UID and the door treats it as a different card. That is also why access never rests on the data: it would be copied along.`,preview:{widget:`door`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`R8`,track:`rfid`,title:`Card friends`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`A friend lives on every card`,goal:`A lab for kids, built from R7's parts. Its own firmware (rfid-lab-r8) makes every card the home of a friend: a fox, a robot, a dragon, a penguin, an alien, a cat, a frog or a unicorn. Tap a card and its friend pops out on the board's screen with stars in its colours, and in 3D in a small React app (esp-labs/apps/friends-app), with its own move, sparkles, sky and tune. Card 1 and card 2 show different friends; tap both and they play together. A new card adds itself on its first tap, and you drag a friend onto it. The app has two tabs: Play for the kids, Setup for the grown-up (real or pretend board, reader health, the card list, sound, calm motion, day or night).`,goalLine:`Give the white card and the key tag a friend each, then tap them and watch both friends play.`,goalCheck:[`The app's Setup tab shows <b>live</b>, the reader chip B2h, and the card count going up as you tap new cards.`,`A new card shows a wobbling <b>?</b> on the screen and “New card! Who lives here?” in the app.`,`After dragging a friend onto it, the friend pops out on the screen and in 3D at the same moment.`,`Tapping card 1, then card 2, puts two different friends on the 3D stage, each on its own numbered card.`,`Poking a friend on the 3D stage makes it play its move again.`],learn:[`UID as a key`,`auto-enrolment`,`state on the board, show in the app`,`WebSocket push`,`drag and drop with pointer events`,`three.js toon shading`,`Web Audio`,`accessible alternatives (tap, keyboard)`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`RC 3.3V`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`15`,role:`RC SDA`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`16`,role:`RC SCK`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`17`,role:`MOSI`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`18`,role:`MISO`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`14`,role:`RC RST`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`13`,role:`IRQ`,side:`L`,kind:`rfid`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`RC GND`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r8 -e rfid-lab-r8 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/rfid-lab-r8 -e rfid-lab-r8 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`any`,does:`Flashes the friends firmware. It uses R7's web libraries and the Wi-Fi network W1/W2 saved.`,see:`<b>FRIENDS stage</b>, a breathing card outline and “Tap a card!”, <code>http://bench.local</code> at the bottom once Wi-Fi joins.`,fix:`“no Wi-Fi saved: run W1 once”: flash W1, <code>join</code>, then R8 again.`},{command:`token`,card:`any`,does:`Prints the admin token (the same one W2 and R7 use). Moving a friend onto a card needs it.`,see:`<code>Admin token: 3f…</code>`,fix:`Keep it out of chats and git.`},{command:`cd esp-labs/apps/friends-app && cp .env.example .env`,where:`mac`,card:`any`,does:`<code>BENCH_URL=http://bench.local</code> (or the IP from <code>status</code>), <code>BENCH_TOKEN=</code> the token.`,see:`A git-ignored .env next to package.json.`,fix:``},{command:`pnpm install, then pnpm dev`,where:`mac`,card:`any`,does:`Starts the app on port 5175 (it can run next to the door-app). Vite forwards /api and /ws to the board and adds the token. A tablet on the same Wi-Fi can open it on the Mac's address.`,see:`<code>http://localhost:5175</code>: Play and Setup tabs, “board live” at the top.`,fix:`No board at hand? Setup → <b>Pretend board</b> plays by the same rules in the browser.`},{command:`tap a card it hasn't seen`,where:`hands`,card:`move`,does:`The board adds it as the next card (up to 12) with nobody on it.`,see:`Screen: a wobbling ? card, “New card! Give it a friend”. App: a new tile calling for a friend.`,fix:`“12 cards!”: forget one in Setup, or serial <code>forget 3</code>.`},{command:`drag a friend onto the card`,where:`app`,card:`any`,does:`Or tap the friend, then the card (or Enter, Enter with the keyboard). Sends <code>PUT /api/card?uid=… {"character":"robot"}</code>, then shows it. Serial: <code>give 2 robot</code>.`,see:`The friend pops out on the screen and on the 3D stage; the tile shows its face.`,fix:`401: BENCH_TOKEN is missing or old, restart <code>pnpm dev</code> after changing .env.`},{command:`tap card 1, then card 2`,where:`hands`,card:`move`,does:`Each tap: the friend's move, sparkles, sky and tune. Two cards within 30 s play together; three at most.`,see:`Two friends on the stage, each standing on its numbered card.`,fix:`No sound: touch the page once (browsers need that), or check Setup → Sound.`},{command:`Setup → name on this card`,where:`app`,card:`any`,does:`A name for that friend on that card (up to 15 plain characters). Serial: <code>name 1 Rusty</code>, <code>name 1 -</code> to go back.`,see:`“Hi again! Rusty” on the screen and in the bubble.`,fix:``},{command:`cards, cast`,card:`any`,does:`Lists every card with its friend and visits, and the friends this firmware knows.`,see:`<code>1  9C4F2A1B  fox  Foxy  taps 5</code>`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`Meet the cast`,widget:`friendsCast`,intro:`The eight friends, drawn the way the board draws them (circles and triangles, no pictures in flash). Press one to see what it does. The board stores only its id; the app knows its move, sparkles, sky and tune.`},{title:`Follow one tap, and a new card getting a friend`,widget:`friendsSeq`,intro:`The card only brings its UID. The board decides who lives there, shows it on its own screen and tells every app.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as R1. Flash <code>rfid-lab-r8</code>. The board needs the Wi-Fi network W1/W2 saved.`,`In the monitor: <code>token</code>, into <code>esp-labs/apps/friends-app/.env</code> as <code>BENCH_TOKEN=</code>.`,`<code>pnpm install</code> and <code>pnpm dev</code> in esp-labs/apps/friends-app, open <code>http://localhost:5175</code>. <b>Checkpoint:</b> Setup says live, chip B2h.`,`Tap the white card and the key tag once each. <b>Checkpoint:</b> two tiles in Play, each asking “Who lives here?”.`,`Drag a friend onto each card. Watch the board's screen and the 3D stage together.`,`Tap card 1, then card 2: two different friends play together. Poke one on the stage.`,`Try Setup → Calm motion and Night, then hand the tablet to a kid.`],note:`The friend is not on the card: the board keeps a list (UID → friend id) in its NVS, namespace friends, separate from R7's door list. So any card works, even one you can't write, and moving a friend is instant. The same idea as R7 (the card only brings its UID) in a friendlier costume. The 3D figures are built in code from spheres, cones and boxes with toon shading, so there are no model files, and the board's screen draws the same shapes in 2D.`,preview:{widget:`friends`}}];export{e as rfidLessons};
